[FINAL-TEXT] **Decisive finding — direct evidence on two devices:**

| Device | Interface | Link | State | IP |
|--------|-----------|------|-------|----|
| router4 | eth4 | link_r4_r5 (→ router5) | **DOWN** | 192.168.15.2/24 |
| router4 | eth5 | link_r4_r3 (→ router3) | **DOWN** | 172.25.1.1/24 |
| router3 | eth5 | link_r4_r3 (→ router4) | **UP** | 172.25.1.2/24 ✓ |
| router5 | eth5 | link_r4_r5 | **UP** (connected route seen) | 192.168.15.0/24 ✓ |

Both of router4's uplinks are **DOWN** while the peer ends on router3 and router5 are **UP**. Router3 is completely healthy. The RIP daemon on router4 cannot run on dead interfaces → router4's FIB contains only `192.168.200.0/24` (its downstream) → any packet from pc11–14 destined outside that subnet hits router4, which replies with ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable." This is a **link fault on router4**, not router3.

**Phase 5 — Final Diagnosis:**

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Both uplink interfaces on router4 — eth4 (link_r4_r5, 192.168.15.2/24) and eth5 (link_r4_r3, 172.25.1.1/24) — are in kernel state DOWN.

Mechanism: The topology runs RIP (FRR/zebra) for inter-site routing. With both uplink interfaces down, router4's RIP daemon cannot exchange updates with any neighbor. Router4's FIB therefore contains only its single directly-connected route (`192.168.200.0/24` via eth0). Every packet forwarded from pc11–14 toward any external prefix (172.16.10.x, 172.16.20.x, 172.18.51.x, etc.) arrives at router4 (192.168.200.1), which has no matching route and responds immediately with ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable," producing 100% loss with errors on the source side.

Localization: router4 — eth4 and eth5 are administratively/state DOWN while the peer interfaces on router3 (eth5, 172.25.1.2/24, UP) and router5 (eth5, UP) are healthy. The link_r4_r3 and link_r4_r5 collision domains are intact on the peer side; the fault is entirely on router4's end of both links.

Root cause: Router4's two uplink interfaces (eth4 and eth5) are in the DOWN state, isolating the 192.168.200.0/24 subnet from the RIP domain and every other site. Router3 is healthy and is NOT the cause.

Fix: Bring up the two uplink interfaces on router4: `ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up`. RIP will reconverge within seconds and restore full reachability for pc11–14. The team should not replace router3; router4 is the device requiring corrective action.
=== END ===